From cf7c0478af6fb423b6be62f53b02153f6636ef44 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Felix <52862870+devilAPI@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 22:49:35 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 14/14] ASoC: Axon 7 Hi-Fi headphone path: AK4490, AK8157,
 ISL98608, MI2S in apq8096

New drivers, written from the register use of ZTE's msm-3.18 drivers:
- ak4490: AK4490 DAC, I2S consumer, 32-bit I2S framing, DFS per rate,
  RSTN as the DAPM DAC bit, soft mute plus an optional external mute
  GPIO, dvdd/avdd supplies and PDN, powered only while used.
- ak8157: AK8157 clock generator as a second codec on the link; it is
  the I2S clock provider (rate register, outputs on/off per stream).
- isl98608-regulator: the ISL98608 analog supply as one regulator
  (enable GPIO, then ZTE's 14 register values).

apq8096: MI2S back ends. qcom,mi2s-clock-consumer on a link makes the
CPU DAI a clock consumer (BC_FC) with the port's EBIT clock gated on in
hw_params (IBIT otherwise), off at shutdown; SLIMbus links unchanged.

Axon 7 DT: AK4490 + ISL98608 on BLSP1 QUP3, AK8157 on QUP6 (both resets),
Quaternary MI2S RX on SD1 (TLMM 58/59/61), ISL54405 switch fixed to the
AK4490 and muted by the codec, all PMIC control pins at 1.8 V (S4) as
downstream (they came up on VPH), sound card "ZTE Axon 7".

Tested: card registers, 48 kHz playback in real time, the stream stalls
when the AK8157 clocks are cut, and while playing the DAC, clock,
switch and supply states equal ZTE's.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
